  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nModest forcing from an axis of mid-level frontogenesis within \nthe right entrance of a 90 knot mid-level jet streak over \nsouthern Wisconsin into extreme northern Illinois is supporting \na band of rain dragging across northern Illinois early this \nafternoon. Precip intensity aloft has been strong enough to \novercome a fairly dry low-level airmass, but is slowly eroding \nthe rain shield with southeast extent. As better forcing remains\nnorth before shifting eastward early this evening, the precip \nband should gradually weaken. \n\nIn the wake of this afternoon/evening's precip, high pressure \nwill build across Wisconsin and Lower Michigan tonight. With \nclearing skies, light winds, and PWATs under 0.5\" tonight across\nnorthern Illinois, temps are expected to fall into the mid to \nupper 40s across interior northern Illinois. Farther south where\nmid-level cloud cover and slightly stronger low-level gradient \nflow lingers well into the night, temps will settle in the low \nto mid 50s. Precip is not expected, though shallow lake effect \nclouds may advect across northwest Indiana tonight and northeast\nIllinois late tonight into Monday morning.\n\nAs a broad mid-level trough lifts northeastward across the \nUpper Mississippi River Valley, a weak disturbance currently \nover northeast New Mexico will phase into the far southeastern \nextent of the northern trough late tonight into Monday. Though \nthe air mass will remain quite dry in the lowest 7-9kft, \nmarginal lapse rates in the 10-20kft layer and modest mid-level \nmoisture advecting northeastward ahead of a building ridge could\nyield a few high-based showers during the late afternoon across\nprimarily the northwest half of the CWA. \n\nAn expansive surface low across far southern Canada will drag a\nslowing cold front across northern Illinois late Monday night \nthrough Tuesday, with consensus guidance stalling the front \nsomewhere across the southern half of Illinois by Tuesday night.\nAhead of the front on Tuesday, temps may push well into the 80s\nand even lower 90s, with increasing low-level moisture yielding\nheat index values nearing 100 across the far southern CWA. \nStrong WSW flow aloft and generally uncapped conditions south of\nthe front in the afternoon would support organized strong to \nsevere convection, but with no clear mid/upper level forcing \naligning atop the surface front, coverage of convection may \nremain low. Torrential rainfall is also possible with any \nconvection as pooling low-level moisture pushes PWATs to near or\npossibly over 2 inches.\n\nWith a broad subtropical high settling over the southeast CONUS\nmid to late week, the overall mid and upper-level flow over the\nwestern Great Lakes will remain generally zonal. The front will\nremain draped across Illinois during this period, though \nwhether it drifts back north over the forecast area at any point\nremains unclear. Either way, periods of scattered showers and \npossibly some thunderstorms can be expected in the general \nvicinity of the low-level front through at least Thursday. \n\nBy Friday, and especially into next weekend, weak mid-level \nblocking will develop as a sub-tropical low over the Gulf \nslows/stalls south of the deamplifying ridge. A trough crossing \nthe northern Great Plains on Friday may extend far enough south \nto yield showers and storms in the area on Saturday.\n\nKluber",
